  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The Sundance Institute is a Utah-based nonprofit organization founded by Robert Redford in 1981 to discover, support, and foster independent film and media artists globally. It hosts the annual Sundance Film Festival, alongside year-round labs, grants, and mentorship programs for filmmakers, documentalists, and episodic creators. Key aspects of the Sundance Institute include: Artist Support & Labs: The Institute operates labs and grants to support independent artists, including the Feature Film Program, Documentary Film Program, and Composer Lab. Sundance Film Festival: The premier showcase for new American and international independent films. Programs for New Talent: The Ignite Program supports creators aged 18–25, while the Indigenous Program supports Native American filmmakers. Sundance Collab: A digital space offering online workshops, classes, and community networking for storytellers. Granting & Fellowships: Provides financial support, including grants for development, production, and editing, such as the Sundance Documentary Fund. The organization focuses on fostering independence, risk-taking, and new voices in storytelling.",
